    detailedExplanation: "Article 21A is one of the greatest transformative achievements in Indian constitutional history.\n\n### 1. The Judicial Genesis\nOriginally, education was situated in Part IV under **Article 45** as a non-justiciable Directive Principle.\n- **Mohini Jain v. State of Karnataka (1992):** The Supreme Court held that the 'right to education' is directly emanative from the right to life under Article 21, and struck down exorbitant 'capitation fees' charged by private medical colleges.\n- **Unni Krishnan, J.P. v. State of Andhra Pradesh (1993):** A 5-judge Constitution Bench tempered Mohini Jain, holding that every child has a fundamental right to free education until they complete the age of 14 years, after which the right is circumscribed by the economic capacity and development of the State.\n\n### 2. The 86th Constitutional Amendment, 2002\nIn response to Unni Krishnan, Parliament enacted the 86th Amendment, creating a tripartite constitutional architecture:\n1. Inserted **Article 21A** in Part III (Fundamental Right for ages 6–14);\n2. Substituted **Article 45** in Part IV (Directive Principle for early childhood care below age 6);\n3. Added Clause (k) to **Article 51A** in Part IVA (Fundamental Duty of parents/guardians to provide educational opportunities).\n\n### 3. The RTE Act & The Private School Quota Controversy\nThe Right to Free and Compulsory Education Act, 2009 came into force on 1 April 2010. Section 12(1)(c) mandated private unaided schools to admit at least 25% students from weaker sections, with the State reimbursing tuition costs.\nIn *Society for Unaided Private Schools of India v. Union of India (2012)* and *Pramati Educational and Cultural Trust (2014)*, the Supreme Court upheld the constitutional validity of Section 12(1)(c), holding that it does not infringe the freedom of trade under Article 19(1)(g), but clarified that minority schools (aided or unaided) are exempt under Article 30(1).",
