    detailedExplanation: "Part IVA was introduced during the 1975–77 emergency to balance the heavy emphasis on Fundamental Rights with civic and constitutional duties.\n\n### 1. Recommendations of the Swaran Singh Committee (1976)\nThe Committee proposed including duties in the Constitution and recommended that Parliament should have the power to penalize non-compliance. While Parliament adopted the duties via the 42nd Amendment, it did NOT include provisions for direct penalties, leaving enforcement to ordinary statutory enactments.\n\n### 2. The 11th Duty (86th Amendment, 2002)\nWhen Parliament enacted Article 21A (Right to Education), it completed the constitutional circle by adding **Clause (k) to Article 51A**, creating a duty on every parent or guardian to provide educational opportunities to their child between the ages of 6 and 14 years.\n\n### 3. Judicial Deployment in Constitutional Review\nAlthough non-justiciable per se, the Supreme Court has creatively utilized Article 51A in public law:\n- **Interpretive Compass for Reasonableness:** In *State of Gujarat v. Mirzapur Moti Kureshi Kassab Jamat (2005)*, a 7-judge Constitution Bench held that when determining the reasonableness of restrictions under Article 19(2)–(6), the Directive Principles in Part IV and Fundamental Duties in Part IVA must be read together.\n- **Environmental Protection:** In *M.C. Mehta v. Union of India*, the Court held that Article 51A(g) read with Article 21 and 48A imposes an obligation on the State and municipal bodies to prevent pollution and protect public health.\n- **National Symbols:** In *Bijoe Emmanuel (1986)*, the Court held that respectful standing during the National Anthem satisfies Article 51A(a); compelling singing violates Article 19(1)(a).\n- **Merit in Public Employment:** In *AIIMS Students' Union v. AIIMS (2002)*, the Supreme Court held that Article 51A(j) (striving towards excellence) is a relevant constitutional consideration in assessing the validity of institutional quotas in super-specialty education.",
    examples: [
      `Environmental Public Interest Litigation: A citizen files an environmental PIL under Article 32 against the discharge of untreated industrial effluents into a holy river. The Supreme Court invokes Article 51A(g) (duty to protect and improve the natural environment) read with Article 21 to order the closure of the polluting industrial units.`,
      `Statutory Offence for Insulting National Anthem: A spectator at a cinema hall intentionally burns and stamps on the National Flag during a screening. The individual is prosecuted under the Prevention of Insults to National Honour Act, 1971. The Supreme Court upholds the conviction, observing that the statute gives legislative teeth to the fundamental duty in Article 51A(a).`
    ],
    distinctions: [
      "Fundamental Rights vs Fundamental Duties: Fundamental Rights in Part III are justiciable legal guarantees enforceable against the State; Fundamental Duties in Part IVA are moral and civic duties imposed on individual citizens without direct writ enforceability.",
      "Directive Principles (Part IV) vs Fundamental Duties (Part IVA): Directive Principles are commands addressed to the State to guide policy and legislation; Fundamental Duties are moral obligations addressed to individual citizens."
    ],
